.ce 2
.B
An Image Display and Imaging Spectroscopy Cube Display System:

Pic Works (PW) User's Guide
.R


.ce
by


.ce 3
Noel Gorelick

and

Roger N. Clark



.ce 3
U.S. Geological Survey, MS 964
Box 25046 Federal Center
Denver, CO 80225-0046



.ce
US Geological Survey Open File Report # ________






N. Gorelick: (303) 236-1411    ngorelic@speclab.cr.usgs.gov

R. Clark:    (303) 236-1332      rclark@speclab.cr.usgs.gov




January 14, 1993



.bp
.ce
.B
Introduction
.R
.PP
Pic Works (PW) is an image and imaging spectroscopy cube display
system developed by MercWorks Software (N. Gorelick) under contract
to the USGS (R. Clark).  Design goals were specific to projects
involving imaging spectroscopy at the USGS in Denver, but we have
found that the design is applicable to many image processing tasks.
.PP
Pw has several unique features, including up to 16 image cubes
online at one time, Color overlays where each overlay has color
intensity ranges, and excellent performance with X-terminals across
an ethernet as well as at a local console.
.PP
Pw is an X-windows based program.  You must have an X-windows device
(e.g. a console or X-terminal).  X-terminals do surprising well with this
program, surpassing consoles in some cases (because tasks are
offloaded from the host cpu).
.PP
This manual is derived from a draft writeup of pw functional
elements by Wendy Calvin, USGS.




.B
COLOR AND THE REPRODUCTION OF THIS MANUAL
.R

.PP
This manual is being copied on regular black and white copy
machines (as a cost saving).  However, all the figures are color.
The figures are all available a x-window dumps, as well as the
manual text via anonymous ftp from speclab.cr.usgs.gov.  Simply
login as anonymous, and enter your username@machine_name as the
password.  Then cd to pub/pw and do a binary get pw.manual.tar.Z.
You must then uncompress it and untar it to the directory of your
choice.  There is a program, pwmanfig, which will find the appropriate figure
and display it on your X-window display (you must customize the
script to point to the correct directory).
.PP
To display a figure from
the manual on your X-display, like figure 1a, simply type:

          pwmanfig 1a

and a full color figure will be shown.  To cancel the
figure and make the window go away, simply click your mouse button
on the window.


.bp
.B
STARTING PW
.R
.PP
To start pw type:

         pw &

(the & is not necessary, but since pw starts its own windows, you
probably want to free the current window so you may run other commands).
You may also start pw including file names:

	pw filename1 filename2 ... &

(if you do not include files on the command line, you can add them later
in pw).  You may include up to 16 files on the command line.
.PP
Note that the 16 files that pw can currently have online at one time can
be any mixture of image cubes, images, overlays or color composites.
(Yes, you can have 16 image cubes online and extract and compare spectra
between them!)
.PP
Pw starts with two windows, shown in Figure 1.  The "Main Control
Window" (Figure 1a) contains options for viewing, color control of
overlays, stretching of images, histograms, controls all other windows
and allows you to turn them on and off at any time.  The "Files Window"
(Figure 1b) is a list of files that are accessed, displaying information
about each file, selected overlays, and color composites.


.bp
.B
FILE LOADING
.R

.PP
If you started PW with image filenames on the command line, these files
should appear in the Files Window with the image letter highlighted in
red.  Information at the bottom of the Files Window (header info, image
format and size) is displayed for the image highlighted with a grey bar.
.PP
If you started PW without any images on the command line, the Files
Window has no files.  To add a file, click the left mouse key once
to highlight the desired letter in grey.  A second click will turn the
bar black and allow a filename to be typed in.  File completion (using
the <ESC> key) will work.  When the filename is complete hit return
and the name will be input, the file header (if any) will be read, and
the file letter highlighted in red (Figure 2).  If you entered these files
on the command line, they would be shown here just like you typed them
in internal to pw (Figure 2).
.PP
If working with an image cube, you must select a band number in the
first column to the right of the list.  Use the same method to select
band number, left click to turn the number black, type in a band number
and return to input that number.  If you make a mistake or don't want to
input a file the right mouse key will return to the grey bar.
.PP
To read the image data into memory, click on the red file letter.  The
letter will turn grey while the reading occurs, and then white when the
image is loaded into memory (Figure 3).  Two copies of the image are
stored in memory:  1) the original data (if 16 bit it stays as 16-bit),
and 2) an 8-bit stretched image for the display.  (Note:  this scheme
uses a lot of memory.  A 1000x1000 16-bit image takes 3 megabytes:  2
megabytes for the 16-bit data and 1 megabyte for the display images.
Three such images and a color composite then take up 10 megabytes of
memory.)
.KS
.PP
\fBIMAGE HEADER NOT RECOGNIZED.\fR  If the file header is not
recognized, you can still read the image, but you must describe it
by hand.  Figure 2 shows the header label information.  Click on
each box and enter the correct value:

.in 10n
.nf
Format: byte (8-bit data)
        half (16-bit data)

Org: BSQ (Band Sequential)
     BIL (Band Interleaved by Line)
     BIP (Band Interleaved by Pixel)

Label: the size of the header label in bytes.

Lines: Number of scan lines in the image.  This is
       the number of pixels in the image top to bottom.

Samples: Number of cross-track samples in the image.  This
         is the number of pixels in the image left to right.

Bands: the number of spectral bands in the data set.

Order: sun (Byte order like on a Sun, or HP computer)
           (Most machines are this way.)
       vax (byte order like on a Dec VAX.

.in 0n
.KE
.KS
.PP
\fBSUB-SCENES AND SKIPS.\fR  If you have a very large image, and the
memory requirements overwhelm your computer, you may want to look at
only a subscene, or some fraction of the data.  The bottom section
of the Files Window contains subsetting and skipping options.  After
reading (or setting) the file header, you can change these fields to
subset your image.  Simply click on each box and enter the value you
desire.
.PP
For example, say you have a 10,000 by 12,000 image, and you
want to look at the subscene starting at line 3,500, sample 5,000,
and you want to look at an image 2000 by 1900 on the screen at one
time (say your screen is 1280x1024).  To fit 2000 pixels into 1024
pixels on screen, you must skip every other pixel.  You would then
set the following values:

.in 10n
.nf
Start L = 3500
Start S = 5000
Height  = 1900
Width   = 2000
L Skip  = 1
S Skip  = 1
.fi
.in 0n

Next, you simply load the image.  If you want to look at another
section, simply change the parameters and reload the image.
.KE


.bp
.B
IMAGE VIEWING
.R

At this point images can be displayed by opening the Image View Window
by clicking on the eyeglasses icon in the upper left of the Main Control
Window.  Next place the mouse cursor in the image window (or in the pan
window or the histogram window, both in the Main Control Window) and
type one of the file letters.  You can cycle or toggle images by simply
typing the letter (it is not necessary to type a carriage return).  A
picture of the full image will then appear in the Pan Window and the
Image Window (Figure 4a,b).
.PP
\fBPANNING.\fR The red box on the Pan Window (see Figure 1a) is the "Pan
Cursor."  The Pan Window is a representation of the entire image, and
the Pan Cursor shows the outline of the image that appears in the image
window.  You can roam through the image by placing the mouse cursor on
the Pan Cursor and holding the left mouse button down, dragging the
cursor.  The image window will be updated real time (within the
limitations of the bandwidth of the systems in use).  You may also place
the mouse cursor to any point in the Pan Window, click the right mouse
button and the pan cursor will be placed at that position and that
portion of the image displayed in the image window.  The Pan Cursor
works for any size image and any size image window size.  Theoretically,
given enough memory, you could have a 10,000 by 10,000 image, or larger,
an image window of 1000 by 1000 and roam around the image (such an image
would require 200 megabytes of virtual memory for byte data).
.PP
\fBIMAGE WINDOW SIZE.\fR The picture window size can be adjusted using
the mouse pointer at the corners, or with the mouse over the eyeglasses
in the Main Control Window, click the middle mouse button and the image
window will expand to the full image size (possibly limited by your
X-window manager).  Be cautious using this expansion on large images -
they may overwhelm the entire screen.  A second click on the middle
mouse key on the eyeglasses icon will alternately enlarge the image to
full size (limited by the screen size) and restore the picture window to
its original size, 300 x 300 pixels.
.PP
\fBMAGNIFY.\fR A magnified portion of the picture window can be viewed
either by clicking on the magnifying glass in the Main Control Window, or by
clicking on any point in the Image Window.  A Magnified Image Window
is opened and a red box appears in the Image Window to denote the area
being magnified.  To change the magnification, place the mouse pointer
in the magnified image and type the desired level 1 through 9.
Multiples of 10 can be achieved using *1 *2 etc.  The image can be
shrunk by using -2, -3, through -9.  Factors of ten shrinking can be
done using /2, /3, etc through /9.  The area magnified can
be moved using point and click, drag will not work.  The size of the
Magnify Window can be changed using your window manager controls.
.PP
\fBDN READOUT.\fR At any time the mouse pointer is in either the picture
window or the magnified image window, the line number (L), sample number
(S) and DN value are displayed under the icons in the Main Control
Window.  The DN value is always the original data value.  For
example if the original data are 16-bit, the DN readout is the
unstretched 16-bit values.  When color composites or overlays are
displayed, the DN values for all components in the image may be
displayed by clicking on the DN readout in the Main Control Window.
This opens a new DN Readout Window described later.



.bp
.B
IMAGE STRETCHING
.R

At the bottom of the Main Control Window is a histogram of the intensity
levels in the image.  The vertical scale of the histogram can be
incremented in steps using the up and down arrows on the right hand
side.  The histogram display can be cycled through a basic histogram
(numbers vs intensity, see Figure 4a) labeled FREQ, a cumulative
histogram (Figure 5a) labeled CUMUL, or NONE.  The cumulative display is
useful for image stretching.  The red line that runs diagonal through
the histogram adjusts the contrast of the image.  This is the DN in
(horizontal axis) to DN out (vertical axis) transfer function.  The end
points move only in a vertical direction, but additional points can be
added to design almost any curve shape (e.g.  Figure 5a).  By clicking
the mouse on "ADD" to the left of the histogram, new points are added to
the red line.  Points can be moved or deleted by selecting "MOV" or
"DEL".  You may click and drag points on the transfer curve and the
stretch changes real time.  By selecting "SHIFT", and dragging the mouse
pointer you can move the entire curve as a single unit.  To return to
the beginning straight line, select "CLEAR".  "ALIGN" moves the shifted
curve back to its original position.
.PP
A Gaussian stretch can be done by setting the histogram to cumulative
(CUMUL) and adding points on the red transfer curve to make it follow
the cumulative histogram.  The transfer function shown in Figure 5a is
close to a Gaussian stretch.  A Gaussian stretch seems to lose detail in
the brighter parts of the image.  To maintain some detail, try setting
the transfer curve slightly below the cumulative histogram at the upper
end of the curve.
.PP
Directly above the histogram are two bar scales which control the lower
and upper grey level of the display.  The numbers on the right hand side
display the range of values for the image.  For example, for 16-bit
data, they might be something like 1523 to 9627.  These values are the
minimum and maximum DN found in the image.  Initially, the values on the
left are the same, but can be changed.  For example, if the histogram is
very narrow, and you want to expand it, click on the left hand numbers
and type in new ones.  You will then have to reload the image (the raw
DN's are already in memory, clicking on the red file letter will just
recompute the 8-bit image in memory).  Values less than the minimum DN
are set to display DN of 0, and values greater the maximum DN are set to
display DN 255.  Use the slider bars to get an idea of what you might
want to set these values.  The upper bar adjusts the lower limit.
.PP
For example, to the min and max in Figure 5a is 1 and 254.  Say we use
the slider bars and decide we want the min and max to be 50 to 220.  By
setting the box in the upper left bar scale to 50 (point and click on
the box), all pixels with values less than 50 are set to zero.  Next
click on the lower left box.  We set it to 220 as in Figure 5b.  The
image is not yet changed.  The file letter changes to red.  You must
click on the file letter and then put the new image into the image
window by typing the file letter with the mouse cursor in the image
window.  The histogram from Figure 5a now appears like that in Figure
5c.
.PP
\fBSLIDER BAR BUG.\fR In some cases, the histogram (and sometimes the
image) appears all grey.  This is really a bug in the auto DN scaling.
To get your image and histogram back, simply grab the slider bars and
move the upper bar to the right then all the way to the left.  Move the
lower bar to the left then all the way to the right.


.bp
.B
COLOR COMPOSITES
.R

With three bands from the same scene, color composites can be made.
Load the images and stretch them as desired.  To change the image
displayed in the Image Window, place the mouse pointer in the small
image in the Main Control Window, or on the image window, or on the
histogram, and type the letter associated with the
image you want to view.
.PP
Next, select a letter from the Files Window that has no title, i.e.  so
that a "<NA>" is highlighted in grey (Figure 6a).  On the column of
double boxes on the right side of the Files Window click on the left
column.  The box will turn red, then green, then blue.  Cycle to reach
the desired color for each of the three bands in the composite.  With
the selection of the first color, the title of the highlighted line will
change from "<NA>" to "<composite>" and the letter on the left will turn
red (Figure 6b).  You may also want to select how many colors are to be
in the final image (unless you want to add overlays, you will probably
want to maximize the colors).  To change the number of colors, click on
the used colors box and enter the new number, up to the available
colors.
.PP
To calculate the composite image (Figure 7), click on the red file
letter, then to display the image place the mouse pointer in the
Image Window, or the Pan or Histogram Windows, and type the
letter associated with the composite image.
.PP
In the middle of the Main Control Window is a box with three bar scales
labeled red, green and blue.  These bar scales adjust the color mix
in a color composite.  By moving the box associated with a particular
color, the contribution of that color to the composite image can be
varied (Figure 8).



.bp
.B
COLOR OVERLAYS
.R

.PP
A nice feature of PW is that it handles intensity variations within
overlays.  That is overlays are not simply on/off, but can contain
variations in intensity level.  For example, when overlays are mineral
band depth maps, then the brightness is also indicative of abundance
level.
.PP
First, lets discuss the concept of "colors" in the display.  PW holds
images as 16 bit then calculates an 8 bit image to go to the display
(because generic X-windows is still limited to 8-bit color displays).
As such there are then 2^8=256 grey levels.  In doing color overlays
this number of "colors" or levels, must be shared among the image, the
overlays, and the rest of the screen.
.PP
For black and white image, the eye is not sensitive to 256 grey levels,
or even 128 grey levels.  A reasonable system seems to be setting the
background image to 64 colors and the overlays to 24 (or if you have
many overlays, 12).  (You might not be able to tell the difference with
even 32 grey levels.)
.PP
Currently, pw can have 16 images online (we do plan to expand it).
So you can have one black and white and 15 overlays.  If the black
and white image has 32 grey levels, and say you have 222 colors
available (some colors are used by the window manager and other
windows on your display--pw leaves these colors alone so when you
move the mouse cursor out of the pw windows, the screen doesn't
flash to a new color palette).  Thus you have 222-32 = 190 colors
available for overlays.  For 15 overlays you could have 190/15 =
12.6 or 12 colors per overlay.
.PP
To set the number of colors in an image, whether to be used as a
color overlay, or a grey scale image, click on the file name in the
files window to turn it grey (if you click twice it turns black to
type in a new file--if you do that click the right mouse button to
undo the file entry).  Then move down to the number of colors and
click on the used colors box and type in the number of colors you
want for that image (Figure 9).
.PP
Each overlay can be viewed and stretched as a normal image (e.g.  Figure
10).  If using more than one overlay, for example, mapping many
different minerals, color each overlay using the RED-GRN-BLU slider bars
in the Main Control Window.  Each color can be adjusted individually to
attain the entire chromatic range.  Colors can also be adjusted using
hue, saturation and intensity, rather than the relative proportion of
red/green/blue.  To switch between these methods of color selection
click the mouse pointer on the box which reads "RED GRN BLU" to the left
of the sliding bars in the Main Control Window.  This box will then
display "HUE SAT VAL" so you know which system you are using.
.PP
After the desired overlays have been stretched and colored they can be
displayed over a background image as follows.  First, in the Image
Window, select the background image (type the file letter associated
with the background image, e.g.  file G in Figure 10).  Then, in the
Main Control Window, select the background image so that it is
highlighted in grey.  Then use the mouse pointer to click on the right
most column of double boxes in the Files Window.  As each overlay is
selected this box will turn yellow.  If a box will not turn yellow, you
have run out of colors (in that case you will need to use less colors in
that or other overlays or the base image and reload them).  Finally,
click on "REDRAW" in the Main Control Window, just under the icons.
This will redisplay the background image with the selected overlays
(Figure 11a).  The initial computation takes a few seconds.  To remove
overlays click on the associated yellow box and then on "REDRAW" (Figure
11b).  Toggling overlays on and off is relatively quick (click on the
yellow overlay box, then on redraw) once the initial overlay mask has
been computed.
.PP
If you cannot seem to get an overlay to turn yellow, check the total
number of colors, or the colors used for each overlay.  You cannot use
more than the total number of available colors.  Also be sure of the
order: base image in the Image Window, and the grey bar on the base
image file name in the Files Window, yellow overlay boxes colored,
and finally click on redraw.


.bp
.B
EXPANDED DN READOUT
.R

.PP
If you click on the DN readout box in the Main Control Window
(Figure 1a), you get an Expanded DN Readout Window (Figure 12).
The expanded box is useful for overlays and color composites.  In
the case of Figure 12, the overlays in the image as well as the base
image are shown.  There are two columns: the DN value (in the
original images) and the scaled to physical value column (marked
scaled).  For example, Figure 12 shows the base image, G, has a DN
value of 4499 (16-bit data) at pixel location line 100, sample 124.
The overlay "D" (magenta) has a DN value of 237.
.PP
If you want the DN's scaled to physical values you must enter the
scale factors.  For each box under the "SCALED" column, click on the
box and enter the full scale value.  For example, in image "G", a
reflectance of 1.0 is DN 20,000.  So clicking on the G box and
entering 20,000 will enter the scale factor.  In Figure 12, we see
that the DN of 4499 in image G is a reflectance of 0.2249, and that
the overlay D has a value of 0.9294 (in this case the value is a
correlation coefficient indicating a very high confidence in that
mineral identification).
.PP
The boxes labeled UTM are for future UTM or longitude and latitude
readout (under development).
.PP
The Expanded DN Readout Window can be turned off by clicking on the
DN box in the Main Control Window.



.bp
.B
SPECTRA FROM AN IMAGE CUBE
.R

.PP
Spectra from an image cube can be extracted and displayed using the Main
Control Window icon that resembles an x-y graph (the upper right icon):
the "Plot Window" as indicated in Figure 1a.  Selecting this icon opens
a spectral display window which can be resized using the mouse pointer
on any corner.  First, place the mouse pointer on the lower left box in
the Plot Window that says "CUBE 0" and type the letter associated with
the image cube.  The box will turn black and display the letter you
typed in.  It is important to have one channel of your image cube
displayed either alone, or with appropriate overlays in the picture
window.  The image could be a single band image, like file G in Figure
10, or extract a channel from the cube.  You select a channel in file H
and load the image (see Figure 10).
.PP
Next, in the plot window, select a color to use for plotting the
spectrum.  On the left of the Plot Window, are three columns of squares:
open white boxes on either side of colored boxes.  Click on the Y column
any box to activate that color.  The box will be outlined in that color
when activated (Figure 13).  (The X column is for wavelengths but is not
yet functional.)
.PP
The magnification window is then used for selecting individual pixels
for spectral display.  The X and Y axes scales in the plot window can be
adjusted using the boxes adjacent to the x or y with an up or down
arrow.  Select the box and it will turn black and you can type in the
appropriate limit, followed by return.  Again the right mouse key will
restore the original value, without change.  Selecting the arrowed boxes
themselves will adjust the scales in step increments in the indicated
direction.
.PP
To display individual spectra, select a color as described above, then
click on a pixel from the magnified image window with the left mouse
button.  The selected pixel will turn the same color as its displayed
spectrum.  Data readout on the spectrum occurs automatically when you
put the mouse cursor on the plot.  The readout is simply the position of
the cursor within the plot.
.PP
To display spectra of a region, hold the mouse key down and drag the
rubber band box to outline the desired rectangular region.  Upon
releasing the mouse key all spectra from this region will be displayed
and the associated pixels colored to match the spectra (Figure 14).  To
delete an individual spectrum, place the mouse cursor on the pixel in
the Magnify Window and press the right mouse button.  To erase all the
plots for one color, select the associated plot color and then click on
the "no" icon (this is a circle with a line through it just above the
integral icon).  To simply turn off all spectra of a given color on the
plot, click on the solid color boxes.  Clicking on the same box again
will bring all the spectra back.  The remaining icons in this window
will be available in a future version of PW.
.PP
In order to get a list of pixels extracted in any one color, click the
right mouse button on the active Y-column box in the Plot Window (Figure
15).  In this Pixel List Window, individual spectra can be highlighted
just by clicking on the line containing the pixel coordinates, and then
deleted (and then undeleted).  This way you can see if individual
spectra are plotting away from the general trend.  (The boxes HILITE and
BLOCK are not yet functional.)  Deleted pixels are flagged with a
negative line number (Figure 16).  You can than write the list to an
ascii file by clicking on the write box.
.PP
Note that you can have multiple cubes (up to 16) online at one time!
You can extract spectra from one cube, switch cubes by entering a
file letter in the cube box, and then continuing to extract
spectra, thus overlaying spectra from multiple cubes.


.bp
.B
OTHER ITEMS
.R

.PP
\fBSAVE AND RESTART\FR.  After attaining certain composites or overlays
you may wish to save your current list of images and selections.  Do
this by clicking on the "SAVE" button near the bottom of the Files
Window.  To restore this, for example on entering PW at a later time
(start pw with no file names on the command line), click on "READ" in
the Files Window.  The "DEL" box adjacent to the latter two will delete
the image highlighted in grey from the file list.
.PP
\fBWRITING IMAGES OUT.\fR If you wish to write an image, or a portion
there of, the pencil icon in the Main Control Window will achieve this.
Click on the pencil and a small window opens which allows you to enter
filenames for each of the red, green and blue image planes.  You can
also select a VICAR header, PGM format, or no header (new formats will
be added), and what to write by cycling through the options under
"Header" and "Write".  When ready, click on "GO" or else use "CANCEL" to
quit.  A second click on the pencil icon will also close this window
without writing.  To write a grey scale image, only enter one file name,
e.g.  under red.  Only enter all three file names when writing color
composites, or color overlay images.  The files are currently always
written as byte BSQ stretched images.
.PP
\fBTURNING FILES WINDOW ON AND OFF.\fR Of the remaining icons in the
Main Control Window, the file folder icon will open and close the Files
Window.
.PP
\fBONLINE HELP.\fR  The on line help denoted by the ? icon in the Main
Control Window is not working yet.
.PP
\fBQUITTING PW.\fR To quit PW simply select "QUIT" in the Main Control
Window.


.KS
.B
ESOTERIC FEATURES
.R

.PP
In the Main Control Window, there is a color bar which displays the range
of color intensities in the displayed image.   This bar is in the
middle, next to the box which says "PICK".
Usually this bar is black to white or black to some color, red for 
example.  But you can also display an image as blue to white,
or primrose to turquoise.  Click the mouse on the box at the left side
of the colored bar.  This selects the bottom end (default is black).
Then use the three RGB slider bars to adjust the color of the low
intensities in the image.  Then click on the box at the right end of the
colored pick bar to adjust the the color of the high intensities of the
image.  In this way the image can grade from any color into any other
color.  It should be noted that for overlays, the "dark" end color will
not appear on the overlay, only the color associated with "higher"
intensities.  However, colored overlays can be displayed over a
background image which ranges from any color into any other color.
.KE
